::ENG1_U02_L06_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A hero crosses a sea for medicine. Adaptation: A student crosses floodwater for batteries. Question: What is preserved?{
=A. A character makes a difficult journey to help others.#Correct. Both versions keep the journey and service.
~B. The setting remains an ancient sea.#Incorrect. The adaptation changes the setting to floodwater.
~C. The character searches for treasure.#Incorrect. Neither version is about treasure.
~D. The conflict disappears.#Incorrect. Both versions include difficulty.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A proud warrior ignores advice. Adaptation: A captain ignores a teammate. Question: What changed?{
~A. The theme about pride disappears.#Incorrect. Pride remains important.
~B. Advice becomes unimportant.#Incorrect. Advice remains central.
=C. The character role changes from warrior to team captain.#Correct. The adaptation changes the situation while preserving the pride conflict.
~D. Both texts become informational articles.#Incorrect. Both are literary situations.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A lamp guides travelers. Adaptation: A phone light guides students. Question: Why does the change matter?{
~A. It removes the idea of guidance.#Incorrect. Guidance remains in both versions.
=B. It updates the symbol of guidance for a modern setting.#Correct. A phone light fits the adapted school context.
~C. It makes the adaptation impossible to understand.#Incorrect. The modern object may make it easier to understand.
~D. It changes the story into a recipe.#Incorrect. The adaptation remains narrative.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A queen protects children during battle. Adaptation: A bus driver protects students in a storm. Question: What theme remains?{
~A. Storms and battles are exactly the same.#Incorrect. The situations differ.
~B. Children cause every conflict.#Incorrect. Children are protected, not blamed.
~C. Transportation is the main theme.#Incorrect. The bus is part of the adaptation, not the main theme.
=D. Leaders protect others during danger.#Correct. Both characters act to protect people in their care.
}

::ENG1_U02_L06_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG1.R.11] Passage: Source: A mythic river tests a hero. Adaptation: A difficult exam tests a student. Question: Which comparison is strongest?{
~A. Both texts include water.#Incorrect. The adaptation has an exam, not water.
=B. Both texts show a character being tested by a challenge.#Correct. The challenge changes, but the testing idea remains.
~C. Both texts are about swimming.#Incorrect. The adaptation is about an exam.
~D. Both texts remove conflict.#Incorrect. Both include conflict or challenge.
}

